GAL22V10
UA3BREB3

AB17 AB18 AB19 AB20 AB22 AB23 CCK CCKQ CDAC GALCLK NC11 GND
AB21 UA3SEL UA3B1 UA3B2 UA3B3 UA3B4 UA3B5 UA3B6 UA3B7 UA3B8 SEQ23 VCC

; =====================================================================
; UA3b REBUILD  -  RocHard RH800C DRAM controller (daughterboard GAL)
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; Replaces the original UA3b, which is FAULTY: its access path is gated
; on AB17, so only addresses with A17=1 ever produce a DRAM cycle. Half
; the fast RAM is unreachable and any A17=0 access hangs the CPU (no
; _UA3SEL -> U11 pin18 never enables -> no DTACK). Proven off-board on a
; TL866 and uniform across all four banks. The original is read-protected
; (galduino + poti sweep failed), so this is reconstructed from measured
; behaviour, not copied.
;
; PIN MAP (kicad-cli netlist):
;   1 AB17   2 AB18   3 AB19   4 AB20   5 AB22   6 AB23
;   7 CCK    8 CCKQ   9 CDAC  10 GALCLK 11 GND(input, tied low) 12 GND
;  13 AB21
;  14 UA3SEL -> U11 pin17 (the sole enable for U11's DTACK driver)
;  15 UA3B1 -> UA3a(74LS244) -> _RAMCAS3   (SIMM6/7)
;  16 UA3B2 -> _RAMCAS2                     (SIMM4/5)
;  17 UA3B3 -> _RAMCAS1                     (SIMM2/3)
;  18 UA3B4 -> _RAMCAS0                     (SIMM0/1)
;  19 UA3B5 -> _RAMRAS                      (all SIMMs)
;  20 UA3B6 -> SIMMA9
;  21 UA3B7 -> SIMMA8
;  22 UA3B8 -> UA1/UA2 (74LS158) row/column SELECT
;  23 SEQ23 -> was NC on the board; used here as a sequencer state bit
;  24 VCC
;
; NOTE ON PIN 1: on a GAL22V10 pin 1 is the dedicated clock for REGISTERED
; macrocells. Here it carries AB17, so this design uses COMBINATORIAL
; macrocells ONLY, with self-holding feedback latches for state - the same
; construct the original uses (and the same one seen in UA4 o16/o17/o18
; and U11 o20/o21). No registered outputs anywhere in this file.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; MEASURED (high confidence - from LA captures on the real card)
;   SIMMA8 = row: /AB18   column: /AB17     (row 8/8; column derived by
;   SIMMA9 = row: /AB20   column: /AB19      the pairing rule, 8/8)
;   bank = (AB23,AB22,AB21) - 1 :
;        001 -> CAS0   010 -> CAS1   011 -> CAS2   100 -> CAS3
;   access window (GALCLK low)  ~640 ns
;   mux to column  +80 ns, back to row +440 ns
;   _UA3SEL asserts +400 ns, releases +640 ns
;   CAS low pulse ~100 ns, RAS low pulse ~280-300 ns
;
; DESIGNED (not copied - the original's exact scheme is not recoverable)
;   the refresh cadence, and the RAS/CAS sequencing inside the access
;   window. Refresh in the original is burst-structured (~280 ns pulses
;   in bursts every ~2 us) and interleaves with accesses, so it could not
;   be separated from the captures. Any CBR scheme meeting the DRAM
;   retention spec is valid, so this uses a simple one.
; =====================================================================


; ---- access in progress ---------------------------------------------
; GALCLK (pin 10) is low for the whole access window. UA4 has already
; qualified the address region, so no address decode is needed here to
; decide *whether* to run a cycle - only *which bank*.
; ** THE FIX: nothing below gates on AB17. AB17 is used ONLY as a
;    multiplexed address bit for SIMMA8, which is what it is for. **


; ---- sequencer -------------------------------------------------------
; Three self-holding latches paced by CDAC (7.16 MHz, 140 ns), each
; advancing on the next CDAC half-period, all cleared when GALCLK rises:
;   UA3B8 low  = column phase   (~+70 ns)
;   SEQ23      = CAS phase      (~+140 ns)
;   UA3SEL low = DTACK          (~+210 ns)
; Earlier than the original's +400 ns, which is safe: DTACK may arrive
; early, the CPU simply inserts no wait states. TUNING POINT - if the
; card is unstable, delay UA3SEL by qualifying it with CCK instead.

; *** REV 3 FIX: RAS has NO SELF-HOLD. ***
; Rev 2 tried to add a precharge gap by delaying RAS one CDAC phase, but
; its hold term (/GALCLK & /UA3B5) latched RAS's EXISTING low the instant
; GALCLK fell -- so when a refresh was in progress the gap never appeared,
; which is precisely the case it was meant to fix.
; Measured in u3ab_timing.csv: refresh RAS falls at +660 ns, cycle 2 of a
; `l 200000` starts at +720 ns with RAS still low, no row is latched, and
; the long read comes back shifted by one word.
; Now RAS is driven by SEQ23, which is unconditionally cleared whenever
; GALCLK is high. At the start of every access SEQ23=0, so RAS is forced
; HIGH regardless of what refresh was doing -- a guaranteed precharge.

; SEQ23: precharge-done flag. Clears whenever GALCLK is high (no term
; survives that), sets on the first CDAC low of an access, then holds.
SEQ23.T  = /GALCLK & /CDAC
         + /GALCLK & SEQ23
SEQ23.E  = VCC

; UA3B5 (pin 19 -> _RAMRAS) stage 1. NO self-hold: strictly SEQ23.
/UA3B5.T = /GALCLK & SEQ23
         + GALCLK & /CCK & /CDAC
UA3B5.E  = VCC

; UA3B8 (pin 22) stage 2: HIGH = row, LOW = column. One CDAC phase after
; RAS, so the row address is held past the RAS falling edge.
/UA3B8.T = /GALCLK & /UA3B5 & CDAC
         + /GALCLK & /UA3B8
UA3B8.E  = VCC

; UA3SEL (pin 14) stage 3: DTACK to U11, one CDAC phase after the column
; address is presented. Holds to the end of the access.
/UA3SEL.T = /GALCLK & /UA3B8 & /CDAC
          + /GALCLK & /UA3SEL
UA3SEL.E  = VCC


; ---- SIMM row/column address (MEASURED - do not change) --------------
; Pin drives the INVERSE of the selected address bit; UA1/UA2 (74LS158)
; are inverting too, so the whole SIMM address bus is inverted.
;   UA3B8 high (row)    -> SIMMA8 = /AB18 , SIMMA9 = /AB20
;   UA3B8 low  (column) -> SIMMA8 = /AB17 , SIMMA9 = /AB19
/UA3B7.T = UA3B8 & AB18
         + /UA3B8 & AB17
UA3B7.E  = VCC

/UA3B6.T = UA3B8 & AB20
         + /UA3B8 & AB19
UA3B6.E  = VCC


; ---- RAS is defined above as sequencer stage 1 -----------------------


; ---- CAS per bank (pins 18/17/16/15 -> _RAMCAS0..3) ------------------
; Access: only the addressed bank, during the CAS phase.
;   bank = (AB23,AB22,AB21) - 1
; Refresh: ALL FOUR assert together (CBR refreshes every bank at once -
; this matches the measured ~6.2% duty seen on all four lines).
/UA3B4.T = /GALCLK & /UA3B8 & /CDAC & /AB23 & /AB22 &  AB21
         + /GALCLK & /UA3B4 & /AB23 & /AB22 &  AB21
         + GALCLK & /CCK
UA3B4.E  = VCC

/UA3B3.T = /GALCLK & /UA3B8 & /CDAC & /AB23 &  AB22 & /AB21
         + /GALCLK & /UA3B3 & /AB23 &  AB22 & /AB21
         + GALCLK & /CCK
UA3B3.E  = VCC

/UA3B2.T = /GALCLK & /UA3B8 & /CDAC & /AB23 &  AB22 &  AB21
         + /GALCLK & /UA3B2 & /AB23 &  AB22 &  AB21
         + GALCLK & /CCK
UA3B2.E  = VCC

/UA3B1.T = /GALCLK & /UA3B8 & /CDAC &  AB23 & /AB22 & /AB21
         + /GALCLK & /UA3B1 &  AB23 & /AB22 & /AB21
         + GALCLK & /CCK
UA3B1.E  = VCC


DESCRIPTION

UA3b rebuild for the RocHard RH800C fast-RAM controller.

The original UA3b gates its entire access path on AB17: with A17=0 it
produces no mux, no RAS, no CAS and no _UA3SEL, so the CPU never gets
DTACK and hangs. Alternating 128 KB blocks work and the rest are dead.
This rebuild uses AB17 only where it belongs - as a multiplexed column
address bit for SIMMA8 - so every address in the window runs a cycle.

Reconstructed from logic-analyser captures rather than a fusemap dump;
the original is read-protected. The SIMM address multiplexing and the
bank/CAS decode are measured and should be treated as fixed. The refresh
scheme and the intra-cycle RAS/CAS sequencing are designed to meet DRAM
timing, not copied, and are the parts to revisit first if the card
misbehaves.
